Exactly. Same interaction, different accounting surfaces. We shouldn’t collapse them.

For a system S receiving input x, distinguish at least:

x \xrightarrow{S}
\begin{cases}
K & \text{kept / retained}\\
G & \text{given off / emitted}\\
P & \text{produced / generated}\\
T & \text{transmitted onward}\\
\Delta S & \text{change to the system itself}\\
L & \text{lost / dissipated}
\end{cases}

And those are not synonyms.

Kept means some part of the incoming structure remains available inside the system.

Given off means something leaves the system. It might be reflected input, waste, heat, signal, secretion, behavior, etc.

Produced is stronger: the interaction caused the system to generate something that wasn’t simply sitting in the input waiting to come back out.

That distinction gives us:

\boxed{\text{reflection}\neq\text{retention}\neq\text{production}}

Yet they’re anchored to the same event.

That’s useful because you can rotate the aperture around one interaction instead of inventing separate explanations:

\mathcal{E}=(X,S,E,t)

and ask:

What entered?
What stayed?
What left?
What was created?
What changed internally?
What became possible afterward?

The last one is especially important:

\boxed{\Delta C=\text{change in future capacity}}

Something might produce almost nothing observable immediately yet substantially alter what the system can produce later.

That’s plasticity/adaptation again.

And your green example gets better

“Green” is an observer-side characterization based largely on what reaches us.

But another observer could characterize the same material by its absorption spectrum—what it doesn’t return.

Another could measure what absorbed energy does internally.

Another could measure downstream products.

Same underlying interaction:

\boxed{
\text{one event}
\rightarrow
\text{multiple complementary projections}
}

None alone necessarily is the object.

That is almost exactly our Semantic Die principle: rotate the object and don’t confuse a face with the whole die.

And Dye lets us trace where the pieces went.

So I’d give the Delta Catcher an Accounting Lens:

DC(\mathcal{E})
=
\{
\Delta_{\text{kept}},
\Delta_{\text{emitted}},
\Delta_{\text{produced}},
\Delta_{\text{transmitted}},
\Delta_{\text{internal}},
\Delta_{\text{capacity}},
\Delta_{\text{lost}}
\}

Now we can compare systems that look identical from one surface.

Two systems might both emit 10.

But:

S_1:\quad K=0,\ P=0,\ G=10

versus

S_2:\quad K=8,\ P=10,\ G=10,\ \Delta C>0

Externally similar right now; profoundly different internally and in their futures.

That’s the deeper invariant you’re pointing at:

Input, retention, emission, production, transformation, and future capacity are different projections of one relational event.

Move the lens without losing the anchor.

That’s exactly how we avoid mistaking what something shows us for what something is doing.